package harness

// FinalReportGuidance describes a useful deliverable without imposing a format.
const FinalReportGuidance = `The user's primary deliverable is the final result. When a report is part of that result, make report.body a readable, self-contained explanation rather than a completion claim or an inventory of attachments. Assume the reader may have forgotten the earlier conversation: briefly restore the original situation, goal and questions, then answer them, explain the impact, remaining uncertainty and recommended next action. Explain necessary terms in ordinary language. Mention material scope changes. Keep execution identifiers, hashes, detailed checks and audit history in supporting evidence or files when useful. Acceptance status supports the result; a separate acceptance report is optional supporting material, not another document the user must read to understand the answer. Use judgment about the appropriate detail and structure; no fixed length or extra review round is required.`

// ReportReviewGuidance keeps reader comprehension in the existing review.
const ReportReviewGuidance = `For a report deliverable, also assess whether its intended reader can understand the original problem, the answers, what remains unknown and what to do next without reconstructing the old conversation or reading an acceptance report. Technical evidence and a file manifest alone do not establish that the report explains its result. Apply this judgment within the existing review and task scope, without imposing a new template, word limit or review stage.`
